low.  \n\nSafety: Check opponents\u2019 discards:  \n- Next player discarded W2, W3, W5, W9, T4, T7, etc. W2 was discarded by next player, so it might be safe against next player.  \n- Previous player discarded W4, W1, etc. W2 not recently discarded by previous.  \n- Across discarded W6, W1, etc.  \nW2 appears relatively safe.  \n\nPromising moves:  \n1. Play W2: discards isolated tile, relatively safe.  \n2. Play T6: discards a tile with low chow potential given T4/T7 scarcity.  \n3. Play B1: discards a tile that could connect, but B1 is terminal, might be riskier.  \n\nGiven hand is far from win, primary goal is to avoid dealing dangerous tiles. W2 seems safest and least useful.  \n\nReason:  \nW2 is isolated in the hand after W1 pung and W3-W5 chow are already exposed; it cannot form sequences with remaining hand. It was discarded by next player recently, so it is relatively safe. Discarding it now minimizes risk while not hurting hand\u2019s future flexibility (keeping B1, B4, B5, T5, T6, B2 for possible chows/pairs).  \n\nAnswer: Play W2"}
