could make chow with B3 or B6 (B6: 3 left).  \n- Pair candidates: B2 (I have 2 already \u2013 that\u2019s a pair!), T5 (only 1), T6 (1), T2 (1), B1 (1), B4 (1), B5 (1).  \nSo I already have a pair of B2. That means my 7 tiles = pair (B2\u00d72) + 5 tiles needing to form 2 chows/pungs.  \n\nPossible sets from the 5 tiles (T2, T5, T6, B1, B4, B5):  \nOption 1: Form chows in B suit:  \n- B1\u2011B2\u2011B3 (need B3)  \n- B4\u2011B5\u2011B6 (need B6) or B3\u2011B4\u2011B5 (need B3)  \nBut B2 is my pair, so B2 can\u2019t be used in a chow unless I break the pair.  \n\nOption 2: Form chows/pungs in T suit:  \n- T5\u2011T6\u2011T7 (need T7) \u2013 only 1 left.  \n- T2\u2011T3\u2011T4 (need T3, T4) \u2013 T3: 3 left, T4: 1 left \u2013 possible but needs two draws.  \n\nGiven the exposed melds and remaining tiles, the easiest route is to try for **All Chows** or **All Simples**-like hand, but W1 pung spoils All Simples, so scoring will be low unless I get lucky with Mixed Straight or Half Flush.  \n\n**Discard choice:**  \n- T6: isolated in T suit, no immediate chow potential with T5 (need T7, only 1 left), T2 is far.  \n- B5: with B4, needs B3 or B6 for chow. B3 has 3 left, so B5 is useful.  \n- T2: needs T3\u2011T4 (T3: 3 left, T4: 1 left) possible but longer.  \n- B1: terminal, if discarded it reduces chance for Outside Hand but that\u2019s already unlikely.  \n- B2: my pair \u2013 discarding breaks the only pair, not good now.  \n- B4: useful with B3\u2011B5 or B5\u2011B6 chow.  \n- T5: useful if I get T6\u2011T7 or T3\u2011T4\u2011T5 (need T3\u2011T4).  \n\nSafest discard considering tile counts and opponents\u2019 melds:  \n- Opponents have many T pungs/chows (next player has T1 pung, T3\u2011T4\u2011T5 chow; across has T8 pung).  \n- B suit seems less dangerous: no B pungs shown except B7 pung (next player).  \n- Discarding T6 reduces T suit congestion and is less likely to be punged (T6: 3 left in wall, T7 only 1 left, so T6\u2011T7\u2011T8 unlikely for opponents).  \n\n**Reason:**  \nI need to keep flexibility for B suit chows (B1\u2011B2\u2011B3 or B4\u2011B5\u2011B6) because B3 and B6 are relatively abundant. My pair is B2, so I should keep B1 and B4\u2011B5 for chow chances. T5 can pair with another T5 (3 left in wall) or form chow with T6\u2011T7 (T7 scarce) or T3\u2011T4\u2011T5 (T4 scarce).  \nDiscarding T6 is relatively safe and preserves hand shape toward B chows.  \n\n**Final choice:** Play T6.  \n\n**Answer:** Play T6"}
